    "text": "MAMMA MIA requires a trajectory that is located in the sub surface ocean, this can be from a number of different sources, currently supported sources are:\n\nintegrated slocum glider simulator\ntimeseries NetCDF/Zarr file\nway-points in csv file\n\n\n\nMAMMA MIA can run simplified missions using the slocum glider simulator, glidersim. If the user installs this optional dependency then glider missions can be ran that will create compatible MAMMA MIA input. The glidersim will also use MAMMA MIA to get environmental data to use during simulation for currents and density etc. glidersim is designed to work exactly like programming an real slocum glider which is not particularly intuitive. Therefore MAMMA MIA provides a simplified interface with the current behaviors able to be programmed:\n\nvirtual mooring (try to make continuous dives at same point)\nfollow way-points\n\nSee Simulators section for more information on using the MAMMA MIA interface.\n\n\n\nMAMMA MIA can read netcdf or zarr files that contain time-series data. Examples include BODC NetCDF files generated as output from real glider and ALR missions, but any 3D spatial time-series with time dimension data should work, although new/updated parameters maybe required if the variable names are ones not defined in the relevant parameter entries in parameter.json. See configuration section for more details.\n\n\n\nMAMMA MIA can read csv files that contain a set of way points, these must be again 3D (need to specify depth) and contain a suitable time value. MAMMA MIA will linearly interpolate a trajectory from these way points based on the mission time step. An example being\nTIME,ALATPT01,ALONPT01,ADEPPT01\n15/06/2023 00:00:00,72,30,5\n16/06/2023 00:01:00,73.5,30,5\n17/06/2023 00:02:00,73.75,30,125\n18/06/2023 00:03:00,74,30,325\n19/06/2023 00:04:00,74.8,30,325\n20/06/2023 00:05:00,74.87,30,250\n21/06/2023 00:06:00,74.94,30,125\n22/06/2023 00:07:00,75,30,5\nThe above is a snippet of a virtual ALR mission where it will start at 5 m, dive to 125 m, then to 325 m before rising to 250 m, 125 m, and finally back to the surface at 5m over the course of 7 days. It also changes its position from 72 degrees N to 75 degrees N staying at 30 degrees E.",
